---
import JournalLayout from '../layouts/JournalLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en';
---

<JournalLayout
  title="Peer Review Policy"
  description="Peer review and editorial review policy for Ayamatma: A Journal of Vedantic Studies."
  lang={lang}
>
  <section class="section stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Peer Review</p>
    <h1 class="section-title">Peer Review Policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      Ayamatma separates peer-reviewed research from editor-reviewed essays and reflections.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Review paths</h2>
      <p>
        Ayamatma uses different review paths for different genres. This is deliberate. A research article,
        a translation note, a signed editorial, and a reflective essay should not pretend to be the same
        kind of object.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Research articles</strong> receive independent peer review from at least one qualified
          reader, and preferably two when the topic requires specialized competence. Only work that has
          completed this process is labelled a research article.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scholarly essays</strong> are rigorous signed arguments published without peer review.
          They are labelled as such and are never presented as reviewed research.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Translations and commentaries</strong> may receive textual or editorial review depending
          on the scope of the submission.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vāda, lectures, oral commentary, and visual essays</strong> are reviewed by the path
          appropriate to their content — from editorial reading to independent external review.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dialogues, interviews, and reflections</strong> are editor reviewed unless the article
          page explicitly states otherwise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Editorials</strong> are signed and are not represented as peer-reviewed research.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Criteria</h2>
      <p>Reviewers and editors evaluate submissions for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Clarity of claim and scope.</li>
        <li>Accuracy of textual, historical, or philosophical representation.</li>
        <li>Source transparency.</li>
        <li>Quality of reasoning and handling of objections.</li>
        <li>Respect for living traditions without loss of critical rigor.</li>
        <li>Genre honesty: the article must not overstate its method or status.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Review concerns content, not medium</h2>
      <p>
        Review concerns intellectual content, not medium. A recorded dialogue, lecture, oral argument, or
        visual essay may receive independent external review just as written work can. Where a transcript
        exists, reviewers may review the recording, the transcript, or both.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reviewer competence is not defined by credential alone. Reviewers are selected for demonstrated
        knowledge relevant to the contribution. Academic qualifications may be evidence of competence, but
        are neither sufficient nor always necessary.
      </p>
      <p class="pr-principle">Rigor is required. Credentials are not.</p>

      <h2>Editor-authored work</h2>
      <p>
        Work authored by the Editor-in-Chief will not be accepted through sole self-review. It will be
        handled as a signed editorial, or reviewed by an independent editorial reader or reviewer. The
        article page will disclose the path used.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</JournalLayout>

<style>
  .pr-principle {
    font-weight: 600; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink) !important;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 1rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
